230842.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   44424 Nov 12 23:13 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  134805 Nov 12 23:23 paper_strategy_repair_agent_codex_20261112_230842_round_1.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   47388 Nov 12 23:21 paper_strategy_repair_prompt_round_1.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     331 Nov 12 23:23 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    9184 Nov 12 23:12 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   10035 Nov 12 23:12 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 1275355 Nov 12 23:12 reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261112_230842.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   28759 Nov 12 23:08 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     265 Nov 12 23:12 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     279 Nov 12 23:12 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    2868 Nov 12 23:13 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7130 Nov 12 23:13 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   27684 Nov 12 23:13 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 12 23:08 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     265 Nov 12 23:23 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     271 Nov 12 23:23 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 12 23:08 task -> ../task\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261112_215056_1794520256531228547/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n==== memory-log\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n==== scientific-evidence\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n==== write-paper-standalone\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n==== validate-paper\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n==== generate-concept-figure\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat VENUE.md; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat VENUE.md; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone`; writing skill: `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** Local Residual Scaling for Compositional Conformal Prediction: Benefits and Limits.\n\n**Central question:** When does training-only local residual scaling improve the coverage–reference-mass tradeoff of split-conformal regions for continuous compositions, and what do homoscedastic and covariate-shift controls reveal about its limits?\n\n**Central thesis:** In the evaluated logistic-normal settings, the frozen training-only scale-selection procedure improves measured coverage–reference-mass tradeoffs over global ILR calibration under boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity, provides no consistent homoscedastic advantage, and performs favorably under the tested shift without establishing shifted-distribution validity or a contribution attributable specifically to quantile stumps.\n\nThis is one condition-structured empirical study, not a general evaluation of all conformal methods, a new compositional geometry, or a discovery diary. Dimensions 3 and 10 instantiate the same question. Homoscedastic, boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic, and shifted conditions are complementary evidence under that question, not separate chronological experiments.\n\n## Evidence and Qualification\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently. `downstream_study` is absent. The authoritative qualification entries are:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nThere is **no failed final qualification in the current artifacts**. This does not establish external validity, superiority of individual components, or reliability outside the tested regimes. Unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate self-diagnostics have trust status `unverified`; do not elevate them into evaluator-certified scientific measurements. Writing, review, audit, and revision must independently preserve the authoritative qualification outcome if artifacts change; an unqualified outcome must never be silently reframed as qualification or assumed to be a performance-only failure.\n\nPrimary quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, plus the declared `final.metric.*` sources. The episode grounding object is a pointer: follow its `json_pointer` to the actual records. Six final seeds are recorded: 1009, 2003, 3001, 4001, 5003, 6007. Each contains H3, H10, B3, B10, S3 and S10, two nominal levels, matched methods, fixed subgroups, reference mass, and uncertainty summaries.\n\nImplementation and design were inspected directly in:\n\n- `results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_tail_adaptive_residual_scaling/program_1/src/program.py` and `METHOD.md`.\n- `task/evaluate_final.py` and `task/resources/final/manifest.json`.\n- `task/program_0/src/program.py` for the original ridge-scale comparator.\n\nThe final manifest and program-tree hash identify the frozen evaluated selection. The inspected implementation is documentarily linked to that selection; this strategy did not independently recompute a source-tree hash match. Do not call that linkage newly cryptographically verified.\n\nThe original quadratic-feature ridge scale has source and search evidence, but no matched repeated-final comparator. Global ILR is a different baseline, not a substitute for that original implementation. Do not compare search scores with final scores or attribute a final improvement to replacing the original ridge learner. There are no `study.*` sources, so omit study-style selection deltas and scored/unscored attempt counts. An unscored interaction attempt is missing performance evidence, not a measured loss; its cause is unknown.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Edit Decision\n\nNo resolved edit-context input, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is present. Thus there is no prior manuscript to preserve or replace. The required enum is **recentered**, meaning recentered from the broad source-task/proposal context, not a claim that an unseen manuscript was edited.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve the broad compositional reliability or latent-target story | Reject as the central story: the measured target is a continuous logistic-normal response, not latent multinomial probabilities or classifier label sets. The proposed count-noise bridge was not implemented. |\n| Broaden to a shared bridge/scale/interaction method family | Reject: the branches do not share usable measured scientific evidence. A failed interaction evaluation cannot be a quantitative ablation. |\n| Recenter on controlled local residual scaling | Select: final records support direct comparisons, subgroup diagnostics, uncertainty, negative homoscedastic controls, and a bounded shift stress test. |\n| Claim quantile-stump superiority or universal validity | Reject: selected-component diagnostics and matched final ridge-only comparisons are missing; shift validity is not guaranteed. |\n\nThis choice follows evidence strength rather than recency, prior prose density, or the name of a proposed method.\n\n## Contributions and Method Groups\n\nThe defensible contribution is an explicitly scoped empirical characterization of a complete scale-selection procedure inside a fixed ILR split-conformal construction. Standard conformal rank validity is explanatory context, not a claimed new theorem. Do not assert literature novelty before checking permitted, actually consulted sources.\n\n1. **Training-only local scale selection — proposed.** Describe the full procedure: quadratic-feature ridge log-scale, constant scale, additive quantile-stump log-scale, four ridge/tail blends, training-only three-fold pinball selection, fitting-only alignment and a one-standard-error heuristic. Do not assume the tail component is selected in any particular case.\n2. **Global ILR split conformal — primary baseline.** Same center, ILR norm, splits and rank correction; no covariate-dependent scale. This is the cleanest scale-adaptivity comparison.\n3. **Raw-simplex L-infinity split conformal — baseline.** A grounded alternative representation/region shape. Comparing it with ILR does not independently ablate geometry.\n4. **Coordinatewise Bonferroni — baseline.** Simultaneous coordinate construction with the evaluator's corrected coordinate quantiles at alpha divided by dimension.\n5. **Constant-scale and permuted-residual training controls — supporting.** Constant/global agreement is a numerical invariance check, not an independent method curve. Permutation probes covariate–residual alignment, not the causal contribution of stumps, blending or selection.\n6. **Uncalibrated, full-simplex and singleton-center comparators — supporting supplement.** These clarify calibration and coverage–size interpretation, not the main learned-method comparison.\n\nNo required new experiment, interaction-depth ablation, latent-count bridge, CQR baseline, weighted-shift method or real-data result is proposed.\n\n## Scientific Design and Results Views\n\nUse three named conditions under one thesis:\n\n- **Homoscedastic controls:** little benefit; small aggregate-utility disadvantages relative to global ILR must remain visible.\n- **Boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity:** strongest joint subgroup-error and reference-mass comparison. H and B change boundary concentration and heteroscedasticity together; neither factor is independently causally isolated.\n- **Covariate-shift stress test:** favorable observed local-scale performance, but no exchangeability-based validity conclusion. In S3 the local method uses more reference mass than global ILR; do not promise uniformly smaller regions.\n\nCoverage is coverage of entire continuous composition vectors. It is not class-label prediction-set coverage, probabilistic classifier calibration, or recovery of a latent probability vector. All generated responses are strictly positive. Exact zeros, finite-count noise, real data and neural classifiers are outside this study.\n\nThe center-fit/scale-fit/calibration/test sizes are 1024/1024/512/2048. The center is a separately fitted ridge predictor in ILR coordinates. The learned scale has access only to scale-training covariates and residual norms. Calibration uses normalized residuals and the corrected split-conformal rank. State the conditional-on-training rank argument and explicitly exclude group guarantees and unweighted shift guarantees.\n\n**Reference mass is not volume.** The evaluator uses 128 selected test covariates and 1024 probes per query, half from a Gaussian residual reference in ILR coordinates and half from uniform-simplex draws. Its covariance is training-dependent. This is a common, measured reference-distribution probability, not invariant simplex geometry or a universal efficiency quantity. Clustered query/probe hits also limit interpretation of probe-hit Wilson intervals.\n\n### Main quantitative views\n\n- Compare four methods directly across H/B/S and both dimensions; never use search trajectory plots.\n- Plot empirical marginal coverage separately at nominal 0.95 and 0.90, retaining all six seed values.\n- Plot reference mass separately, with the two nominal levels averaged within seed and clearly labeled; read it alongside coverage, not as an unconstrained size ranking.\n- Keep subgroup evidence in the main paper: a six-condition table of mean absolute subgroup coverage error for local scaling, global ILR and permuted residuals, averaged over both fixed groups and nominal levels. Complete signed per-group/per-level values remain in the supplement.\n- Use an aggregate table for utility and its error/mass decomposition. Utility weights are a chosen evaluation convention, not a universal scientific objective.\n\nGrounded aggregate anchors, rounded appropriately in any future paper:\n\n| Quantity | Value | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local mean utility | 0.8772268474776957 | `final.metric.combined_score` |\n| Stored utility interval | [0.8766236679621646, 0.8781275287778212] | `final.metric.combined_score_ci_low`, `final.metric.combined_score_ci_high` |\n| Mean marginal absolute coverage error | 0.011729600694444453 | `final.metric.marginal_coverage_error` |\n| Mean fixed-group absolute coverage error | 0.015105333854689552 | `final.metric.group_coverage_error` |\n| Mean reference mass | 0.4508402082655165 | `final.metric.reference_mass` |\n| Local-minus-global mean utility | 0.04762325792650014 | `final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap` |\n| Stored paired difference interval | [0.043901240346666696, 0.050616967867983816] | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nVisible manuscript text should call the aggregate quantity **utility**, not its internal field name. Define it as one minus 0.375 times marginal absolute coverage error, minus 0.375 times group absolute coverage error, minus 0.25 times reference mass, following the evaluator. Control checks all pass here, so the recorded aggregate agrees with this interpretation.\n\n### Uncertainty discipline\n\nThe evaluator uses 2000 paired seed-bootstrap resamples over the fixed six-condition grid. The repeated identical aggregate paired intervals embedded in episodes are one estimate, not six independent estimates. Stored Wilson intervals describe within-seed hit counts, not variability across refitted predictors. Do not average interval endpoints, attach overall bootstrap bounds to individual conditions, pool all test points as one fitted model, or infer independent-query uncertainty from clustered probe intervals. Use seed dots rather than inventing unrecorded condition-specific confidence intervals. Six seeds constrain precision and generalizability.\n\n## Figures and Readability\n\nInspected the ICLR template at `.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex` and `paper.sty`. The practical text block is **single-column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Full width is therefore the same as the sole column width, not a two-column conference span. All required figures use the full text width. Never rescue density by shrinking labels.\n\n### Figure 1 — Conceptual overview\n\n- **Required; generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; the only overview assigned to that workflow.\n- **Purpose:** interior-simplex response → ILR center and training-only positive scale → held-out normalized-score calibration → separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation. A short callout places shift outside the exchangeability guarantee.\n- **Footprint:** page 1 or 2, approximately 5.5 × 1.9 inches, about 0.25 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** one panel, one panel per row, at most six labeled blocks, no more than four visual series; 9-point labels or larger.\n- **Safety:** conceptual only, no invented observed points, numeric results or data-shaped example regions. A workflow schematic explains the method, not discovery order.\n\n### Figure 2 — Coverage Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; faceted seed-dot plot.\n- **Panels:** 2 × 2, dimension by nominal level. Each panel has H/B/S and four methods: local scaling, global ILR, raw L-infinity and Bonferroni.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 4.2 inches, about 0.55 page including caption.\n- **Budget:** four total panels, two per row, three categories per panel, four series. Six small offset seed dots per method and an arithmetic-mean marker; one shared legend.\n- **Readability:** common coverage axis, horizontal nominal target, label text at least 9 points and ticks at least 8 points. No lines implying a trajectory through H/B/S. Show existing seed variability without fabricated condition-specific intervals.\n\n### Figure 3 — Reference Mass Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; two dimension panels with the same four methods and three condition categories.\n- **Metric:** within-seed mean reference mass over the two nominal levels. Caption names that averaging and the mixed reference measure.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 2.5 inches, about 0.35 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** two panels in one row, three categories per panel, four series; seed dots and mean markers, shared scale and legend, same typography as Figure 2.\n- **Readability:** do not merge Figures 2 and 3 into a dense six-panel figure. Pair this size view with the coverage plot and main subgroup table.\n\n**Export contract:** quantitative plotting source must export both PDF and PNG, with PNG at 300 DPI, and LaTeX must reference the PDF when present. Raster-only full-width assets require regeneration at least 2400 pixels wide; any later narrower column-width-only raster requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale an existing image. Preserve editable concept source and generator provenance; document an actual fallback if needed rather than silently replacing the overview. No figure is generated at this stage.\n\n## Main-Paper Structure and Page Budget\n\nTarget **8.5 substantive body pages**, satisfying the venue's 8–9-page rule; if the body ends at 8 pages, its last page must be at least 75% occupied. Allocations include associated floats:\n\n| Component | Pages | Scientific value |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Abstract and Introduction, including early overview | 1.25 | Target and question, bounded contributions, glance-value explanation |\n| Related Work | 0.65 | Consulted foundations, ILR context and distinction from neighboring quantile methods |\n| Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning | 1.85 | Complete procedure, center/scale/calibration separation, short conditional rank argument |\n| Evaluation Design | 1.15 | Generator, comparators, groups, reference measure and uncertainty |\n| Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions | 2.40 | Two quantitative figures, aggregate table and subgroup table; negative controls remain visible |\n| Discussion and Limitations | 0.95 | Confounded condition changes, missing attribution, shift and external-validity limits |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 | Direct answer within the tested scope |\n| **Total** | **8.50** | No generic filler or chronology |\n\nSubheadings in results should identify **Homoscedastic Controls**, **Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity**, and **Covariate-Shift Stress Test**. If space remains, improve the exact reference-measure explanation, scale-selection description and subgroup contrasts. If space is tight, move extended pseudocode after references—not the failed/negative controls or central limitations.\n\nTable 1 compares the four main methods' aggregate utility, errors and mass, with stored paired local-minus-comparator intervals explicitly labeled as aggregate. Table 2 compares local/global/permuted subgroup errors across the six conditions. Do not turn the original ridge-scale code into a nonexistent final-results column.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nPurpose: substantive reconstructability and complete observed diagnostics, not a hiding place for evidence essential to the thesis. No speculative future ablation or posthoc ranking is assigned. Separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses should also go here without retroactively altering this strategy.\n\n1. **Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification.** Source-derived pseudocode and all actual settings: tail quantile 0.9, 15 threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 updates, three deterministic folds, four blend weights, fitting-only alignment, simplicity order, refit and numerical clipping. The one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a test. Do not invent component-selection frequencies. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary sources are the selected implementation and `METHOD.md`, not unverified candidate diagnostics.\n2. **Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure.** Full equations from `task/evaluate_final.py`, six-dimensional covariates, shift 1.25, dimensions, split sizes, ridge center, ILR basis, strict positivity, probe mixture and query-clustering limits. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary source: final resource manifest and evaluator.\n3. **Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables.** All cases/seeds/levels/groups for the four main methods, signed coverage deviations, reference mass and recorded Wilson intervals. Describe aggregate bootstrap intervals once. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.marginal_coverage_error`, `final.metric.group_coverage_error`, `final.metric.reference_mass`, `final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap`.\n4. **Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks.** Constant/global agreement, permuted residuals, uncalibrated quantiles, full-simplex and singleton-center checks; explain exactly what each tests. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.constant_control_score`, `final.metric.permuted_control_score`, `final.metric.control_pass_fraction`.\n5. **Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries.** Standard rank proof conditional on center/scale training, corrected rank with infinity when necessary, ties and common scale clipping. Not a new theorem, subgroup guarantee or shift theorem. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; construction details come from the evaluator and selected method source.\n6. **Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope.** Recorded Python 3.12/NumPy 2.1.3 statements, final seeds, synthetic generation, anonymous relative commands, hashes and figure/table-generator mapping when implemented. Stored per-seed elapsed durations are not new method-only runtime measurements. Disclose unimplemented count-noise work, the unscored interaction extension, unknown failure cause, absent matched final ridge-scale evidence, and omitted study-style search counts/deltas. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.*`; process facts require their actual local records, not inference from an episode pointer.\n\n### Separate Required Disclosure\n\n**LLM Usage.** Retain this separate mandatory section after references, outside the six evidence-backed supplementary subsections listed in JSON. Disclose assistance evidenced by the current framing interaction and available proposal, source-editing and reference-query process artifacts; add the precise writing role when it occurs. Inspect actual logs before attributing further roles or model versions. Do not claim unrecorded manual checks or call unexecuted experiments completed. Process provenance is not grounded in a scientific-result episode: do not invent a result source ID to support this disclosure. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if documented. This strategy made no network requests.\n\n## Literature, Anonymity and Writing Safeguards\n\n`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` exist. Retrieval metadata is not evidence that a paper was read or even relevant. `Lei2016DistributionFreePI` is a directly relevant metadata match in the allowed starter lineage; actual consulted content still requires verification before citation. Do not pad related work with irrelevant retrieval results. Verify appropriate ILR, quantile-learning and conditional-coverage sources through permitted material before making novelty claims. Do not search for or identify the prohibited related paper, expand citation networks into it, or read citing/discussing pages. Stop and document an actual accidental encounter.\n\nUse ICLR 2026 as required by `VENUE.md`. Do not claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release. The inspected scaffold contains a named author and final-copy switch: those must not be copied into the anonymous review manuscript. Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links. Preserve automatically managed watermark blocks. Include `LLM Usage` and any actually required ICLR checklist, not an invented checklist borrowed from another venue.\n\n### Forbidden Headings\n\nSearch Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Iteration History; Experiment A; Experiment B; Winner Selection; From Failure to Success; Latent-Count Bridge Results; Quantile-Stump Ablation.\n\n### Forbidden Phrases\n\n“we then tried”; “subsequent improvements”; “first valid result”; “next-best”; “later variants”; “search winner”; “correctness rate”; “correct and eligible”; “eligible”; “combined_score”; “rubric”; “worker_01”; “worker_02”; “state of the art”; “universally reliable”; “uniformly smaller regions”; “guaranteed coverage under covariate shift”; “distribution-free conditional validity”; “quantile stumps caused the gain”; “the latent bridge restored coverage”; “unscored attempts were measured losses”.\n\nInternal field names and pipeline labels in this memo are **private-only grounding context**, never recommended visible manuscript terminology. Memory is private qualitative context, not numerical grounding. Old implementation-stage statements of incomplete execution must not override completed final evaluation evidence.\n\n## Stage Boundary\n\nThis strategy writes only the framing memo and JSON, with append-only memory updates. It does not draft `paper.tex`, write `claims_grounding.json`, generate figures, run experiments, or run final-paper validation. Strategy-schema, grounding-ID and consistency checks are appropriate here. The final writer must preserve the scope, qualification facts, negative controls and limitations throughout drafting and audit.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,180p' paper_strategy.md; cat references.bib; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json; command -v apply_patch; ls .codex/skills/*/scripts\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,180p' paper_strategy.md; cat references.bib; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json; command -v apply_patch; ls .codex/skills/*/scripts\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone`; writing skill: `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** Local Residual Scaling for Compositional Conformal Prediction: Benefits and Limits.\n\n**Central question:** When does training-only local residual scaling improve the coverage–reference-mass tradeoff of split-conformal regions for continuous compositions, and what do homoscedastic and covariate-shift controls reveal about its limits?\n\n**Central thesis:** In the evaluated logistic-normal settings, the frozen training-only scale-selection procedure improves measured coverage–reference-mass tradeoffs over global ILR calibration under boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity, provides no consistent homoscedastic advantage, and performs favorably under the tested shift without establishing shifted-distribution validity or a contribution attributable specifically to quantile stumps.\n\nThis is one condition-structured empirical study, not a general evaluation of all conformal methods, a new compositional geometry, or a discovery diary. Dimensions 3 and 10 instantiate the same question. Homoscedastic, boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic, and shifted conditions are complementary evidence under that question, not separate chronological experiments.\n\n## Evidence and Qualification\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently. `downstream_study` is absent. The authoritative qualification entries are:\n\n- `final.qualification.status`: `completed`.\n- `final.qualification.outcome`: `qualified`.\n- `final.qualification.correct`: `true`.\n\nThere is **no failed final qualification in the current artifacts**. This does not establish external validity, superiority of individual components, or reliability outside the tested regimes. Unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate self-diagnostics have trust status `unverified`; do not elevate them into evaluator-certified scientific measurements. Writing, review, audit, and revision must independently preserve the authoritative qualification outcome if artifacts change; an unqualified outcome must never be silently reframed as qualification or assumed to be a performance-only failure.\n\nPrimary quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, plus the declared `final.metric.*` sources. The episode grounding object is a pointer: follow its `json_pointer` to the actual records. Six final seeds are recorded: 1009, 2003, 3001, 4001, 5003, 6007. Each contains H3, H10, B3, B10, S3 and S10, two nominal levels, matched methods, fixed subgroups, reference mass, and uncertainty summaries.\n\nImplementation and design were inspected directly in:\n\n- `results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_tail_adaptive_residual_scaling/program_1/src/program.py` and `METHOD.md`.\n- `task/evaluate_final.py` and `task/resources/final/manifest.json`.\n- `task/program_0/src/program.py` for the original ridge-scale comparator.\n\nThe final manifest and program-tree hash identify the frozen evaluated selection. The inspected implementation is documentarily linked to that selection; this strategy did not independently recompute a source-tree hash match. Do not call that linkage newly cryptographically verified.\n\nThe original quadratic-feature ridge scale has source and search evidence, but no matched repeated-final comparator. Global ILR is a different baseline, not a substitute for that original implementation. Do not compare search scores with final scores or attribute a final improvement to replacing the original ridge learner. There are no `study.*` sources, so omit study-style selection deltas and scored/unscored attempt counts. An unscored interaction attempt is missing performance evidence, not a measured loss; its cause is unknown.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Edit Decision\n\nNo resolved edit-context input, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is present. Thus there is no prior manuscript to preserve or replace. The required enum is **recentered**, meaning recentered from the broad source-task/proposal context, not a claim that an unseen manuscript was edited.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve the broad compositional reliability or latent-target story | Reject as the central story: the measured target is a continuous logistic-normal response, not latent multinomial probabilities or classifier label sets. The proposed count-noise bridge was not implemented. |\n| Broaden to a shared bridge/scale/interaction method family | Reject: the branches do not share usable measured scientific evidence. A failed interaction evaluation cannot be a quantitative ablation. |\n| Recenter on controlled local residual scaling | Select: final records support direct comparisons, subgroup diagnostics, uncertainty, negative homoscedastic controls, and a bounded shift stress test. |\n| Claim quantile-stump superiority or universal validity | Reject: selected-component diagnostics and matched final ridge-only comparisons are missing; shift validity is not guaranteed. |\n\nThis choice follows evidence strength rather than recency, prior prose density, or the name of a proposed method.\n\n## Contributions and Method Groups\n\nThe defensible contribution is an explicitly scoped empirical characterization of a complete scale-selection procedure inside a fixed ILR split-conformal construction. Standard conformal rank validity is explanatory context, not a claimed new theorem. Do not assert literature novelty before checking permitted, actually consulted sources.\n\n1. **Training-only local scale selection — proposed.** Describe the full procedure: quadratic-feature ridge log-scale, constant scale, additive quantile-stump log-scale, four ridge/tail blends, training-only three-fold pinball selection, fitting-only alignment and a one-standard-error heuristic. Do not assume the tail component is selected in any particular case.\n2. **Global ILR split conformal — primary baseline.** Same center, ILR norm, splits and rank correction; no covariate-dependent scale. This is the cleanest scale-adaptivity comparison.\n3. **Raw-simplex L-infinity split conformal — baseline.** A grounded alternative representation/region shape. Comparing it with ILR does not independently ablate geometry.\n4. **Coordinatewise Bonferroni — baseline.** Simultaneous coordinate construction with the evaluator's corrected coordinate quantiles at alpha divided by dimension.\n5. **Constant-scale and permuted-residual training controls — supporting.** Constant/global agreement is a numerical invariance check, not an independent method curve. Permutation probes covariate–residual alignment, not the causal contribution of stumps, blending or selection.\n6. **Uncalibrated, full-simplex and singleton-center comparators — supporting supplement.** These clarify calibration and coverage–size interpretation, not the main learned-method comparison.\n\nNo required new experiment, interaction-depth ablation, latent-count bridge, CQR baseline, weighted-shift method or real-data result is proposed.\n\n## Scientific Design and Results Views\n\nUse three named conditions under one thesis:\n\n- **Homoscedastic controls:** little benefit; small aggregate-utility disadvantages relative to global ILR must remain visible.\n- **Boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity:** strongest joint subgroup-error and reference-mass comparison. H and B change boundary concentration and heteroscedasticity together; neither factor is independently causally isolated.\n- **Covariate-shift stress test:** favorable observed local-scale performance, but no exchangeability-based validity conclusion. In S3 the local method uses more reference mass than global ILR; do not promise uniformly smaller regions.\n\nCoverage is coverage of entire continuous composition vectors. It is not class-label prediction-set coverage, probabilistic classifier calibration, or recovery of a latent probability vector. All generated responses are strictly positive. Exact zeros, finite-count noise, real data and neural classifiers are outside this study.\n\nThe center-fit/scale-fit/calibration/test sizes are 1024/1024/512/2048. The center is a separately fitted ridge predictor in ILR coordinates. The learned scale has access only to scale-training covariates and residual norms. Calibration uses normalized residuals and the corrected split-conformal rank. State the conditional-on-training rank argument and explicitly exclude group guarantees and unweighted shift guarantees.\n\n**Reference mass is not volume.** The evaluator uses 128 selected test covariates and 1024 probes per query, half from a Gaussian residual reference in ILR coordinates and half from uniform-simplex draws. Its covariance is training-dependent. This is a common, measured reference-distribution probability, not invariant simplex geometry or a universal efficiency quantity. Clustered query/probe hits also limit interpretation of probe-hit Wilson intervals.\n\n### Main quantitative views\n\n- Compare four methods directly across H/B/S and both dimensions; never use search trajectory plots.\n- Plot empirical marginal coverage separately at nominal 0.95 and 0.90, retaining all six seed values.\n- Plot reference mass separately, with the two nominal levels averaged within seed and clearly labeled; read it alongside coverage, not as an unconstrained size ranking.\n- Keep subgroup evidence in the main paper: a six-condition table of mean absolute subgroup coverage error for local scaling, global ILR and permuted residuals, averaged over both fixed groups and nominal levels. Complete signed per-group/per-level values remain in the supplement.\n- Use an aggregate table for utility and its error/mass decomposition. Utility weights are a chosen evaluation convention, not a universal scientific objective.\n\nGrounded aggregate anchors, rounded appropriately in any future paper:\n\n| Quantity | Value | Source |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local mean utility | 0.8772268474776957 | `final.metric.combined_score` |\n| Stored utility interval | [0.8766236679621646, 0.8781275287778212] | `final.metric.combined_score_ci_low`, `final.metric.combined_score_ci_high` |\n| Mean marginal absolute coverage error | 0.011729600694444453 | `final.metric.marginal_coverage_error` |\n| Mean fixed-group absolute coverage error | 0.015105333854689552 | `final.metric.group_coverage_error` |\n| Mean reference mass | 0.4508402082655165 | `final.metric.reference_mass` |\n| Local-minus-global mean utility | 0.04762325792650014 | `final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap` |\n| Stored paired difference interval | [0.043901240346666696, 0.050616967867983816] | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nVisible manuscript text should call the aggregate quantity **utility**, not its internal field name. Define it as one minus 0.375 times marginal absolute coverage error, minus 0.375 times group absolute coverage error, minus 0.25 times reference mass, following the evaluator. Control checks all pass here, so the recorded aggregate agrees with this interpretation.\n\n### Uncertainty discipline\n\nThe evaluator uses 2000 paired seed-bootstrap resamples over the fixed six-condition grid. The repeated identical aggregate paired intervals embedded in episodes are one estimate, not six independent estimates. Stored Wilson intervals describe within-seed hit counts, not variability across refitted predictors. Do not average interval endpoints, attach overall bootstrap bounds to individual conditions, pool all test points as one fitted model, or infer independent-query uncertainty from clustered probe intervals. Use seed dots rather than inventing unrecorded condition-specific confidence intervals. Six seeds constrain precision and generalizability.\n\n## Figures and Readability\n\nInspected the ICLR template at `.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex` and `paper.sty`. The practical text block is **single-column, 5.5 inches wide and 9 inches high**. Full width is therefore the same as the sole column width, not a two-column conference span. All required figures use the full text width. Never rescue density by shrinking labels.\n\n### Figure 1 — Conceptual overview\n\n- **Required; generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; the only overview assigned to that workflow.\n- **Purpose:** interior-simplex response → ILR center and training-only positive scale → held-out normalized-score calibration → separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation. A short callout places shift outside the exchangeability guarantee.\n- **Footprint:** page 1 or 2, approximately 5.5 × 1.9 inches, about 0.25 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** one panel, one panel per row, at most six labeled blocks, no more than four visual series; 9-point labels or larger.\n- **Safety:** conceptual only, no invented observed points, numeric results or data-shaped example regions. A workflow schematic explains the method, not discovery order.\n\n### Figure 2 — Coverage Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; faceted seed-dot plot.\n- **Panels:** 2 × 2, dimension by nominal level. Each panel has H/B/S and four methods: local scaling, global ILR, raw L-infinity and Bonferroni.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 4.2 inches, about 0.55 page including caption.\n- **Budget:** four total panels, two per row, three categories per panel, four series. Six small offset seed dots per method and an arithmetic-mean marker; one shared legend.\n- **Readability:** common coverage axis, horizontal nominal target, label text at least 9 points and ticks at least 8 points. No lines implying a trajectory through H/B/S. Show existing seed variability without fabricated condition-specific intervals.\n\n### Figure 3 — Reference Mass Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; two dimension panels with the same four methods and three condition categories.\n- **Metric:** within-seed mean reference mass over the two nominal levels. Caption names that averaging and the mixed reference measure.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 2.5 inches, about 0.35 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** two panels in one row, three categories per panel, four series; seed dots and mean markers, shared scale and legend, same typography as Figure 2.\n- **Readability:** do not merge Figures 2 and 3 into a dense six-panel figure. Pair this size view with the coverage plot and main subgroup table.\n\n**Export contract:** quantitative plotting source must export both PDF and PNG, with PNG at 300 DPI, and LaTeX must reference the PDF when present. Raster-only full-width assets require regeneration at least 2400 pixels wide; any later narrower column-width-only raster requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale an existing image. Preserve editable concept source and generator provenance; document an actual fallback if needed rather than silently replacing the overview. No figure is generated at this stage.\n\n## Main-Paper Structure and Page Budget\n\nTarget **8.5 substantive body pages**, satisfying the venue's 8–9-page rule; if the body ends at 8 pages, its last page must be at least 75% occupied. Allocations include associated floats:\n\n| Component | Pages | Scientific value |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Abstract and Introduction, including early overview | 1.25 | Target and question, bounded contributions, glance-value explanation |\n| Related Work | 0.65 | Consulted foundations, ILR context and distinction from neighboring quantile methods |\n| Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning | 1.85 | Complete procedure, center/scale/calibration separation, short conditional rank argument |\n| Evaluation Design | 1.15 | Generator, comparators, groups, reference measure and uncertainty |\n| Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions | 2.40 | Two quantitative figures, aggregate table and subgroup table; negative controls remain visible |\n| Discussion and Limitations | 0.95 | Confounded condition changes, missing attribution, shift and external-validity limits |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 | Direct answer within the tested scope |\n| **Total** | **8.50** | No generic filler or chronology |\n\nSubheadings in results should identify **Homoscedastic Controls**, **Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity**, and **Covariate-Shift Stress Test**. If space remains, improve the exact reference-measure explanation, scale-selection description and subgroup contrasts. If space is tight, move extended pseudocode after references—not the failed/negative controls or central limitations.\n\nTable 1 compares the four main methods' aggregate utility, errors and mass, with stored paired local-minus-comparator intervals explicitly labeled as aggregate. Table 2 compares local/global/permuted subgroup errors across the six conditions. Do not turn the original ridge-scale code into a nonexistent final-results column.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nPurpose: substantive reconstructability and complete observed diagnostics, not a hiding place for evidence essential to the thesis. No speculative future ablation or posthoc ranking is assigned. Separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses should also go here without retroactively altering this strategy.\n\n1. **Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification.** Source-derived pseudocode and all actual settings: tail quantile 0.9, 15 threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 updates, three deterministic folds, four blend weights, fitting-only alignment, simplicity order, refit and numerical clipping. The one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a test. Do not invent component-selection frequencies. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary sources are the selected implementation and `METHOD.md`, not unverified candidate diagnostics.\n2. **Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure.** Full equations from `task/evaluate_final.py`, six-dimensional covariates, shift 1.25, dimensions, split sizes, ridge center, ILR basis, strict positivity, probe mixture and query-clustering limits. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary source: final resource manifest and evaluator.\n3. **Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables.** All cases/seeds/levels/groups for the four main methods, signed coverage deviations, reference mass and recorded Wilson intervals. Describe aggregate bootstrap intervals once. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.marginal_coverage_error`, `final.metric.group_coverage_error`, `final.metric.reference_mass`, `final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap`.\n4. **Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks.** Constant/global agreement, permuted residuals, uncalibrated quantiles, full-simplex and singleton-center checks; explain exactly what each tests. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.constant_control_score`, `final.metric.permuted_control_score`, `final.metric.control_pass_fraction`.\n5. **Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries.** Standard rank proof conditional on center/scale training, corrected rank with infinity when necessary, ties and common scale clipping. Not a new theorem, subgroup guarantee or shift theorem. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; construction details come from the evaluator and selected method source.\n6. **Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope.** Recorded Python 3.12/NumPy 2.1.3 statements, final seeds, synthetic generation, anonymous relative commands, hashes and figure/table-generator mapping when implemented. Stored per-seed elapsed durations are not new method-only runtime measurements. Disclose unimplemented count-noise work, the unscored interaction extension, unknown failure cause, absent matched final ridge-scale evidence, and omitted study-style search counts/deltas. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.*`; process facts require their actual local records, not inference from an episode pointer.\n\n### Separate Required Disclosure\n\n**LLM Usage.** Retain this separate mandatory section after references, outside the six evidence-backed supplementary subsections listed in JSON. Disclose assistance evidenced by the current framing interaction and available proposal, source-editing and reference-query process artifacts; add the precise writing role when it occurs. Inspect actual logs before attributing further roles or model versions. Do not claim unrecorded manual checks or call unexecuted experiments completed. Process provenance is not grounded in a scientific-result episode: do not invent a result source ID to support this disclosure. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if documented. This strategy made no network requests.\n\n## Literature, Anonymity and Writing Safeguards\n\n`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` exist. Retrieval metadata is not evidence that a paper was read or even relevant. `Lei2016DistributionFreePI` is a directly relevant metadata match in the allowed starter lineage; actual consulted content still requires verification before citation. Do not pad related work with irrelevant retrieval results. Verify appropriate ILR, quantile-learning and conditional-coverage sources through permitted material before making novelty claims. Do not search for or identify the prohibited related paper, expand citation networks into it, or read citing/discussing pages. Stop and document an actual accidental encounter.\n\nUse ICLR 2026 as required by `VENUE.md`. Do not claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release. The inspected scaffold contains a named author and final-copy switch: those must not be copied into the anonymous review manuscript. Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links. Preserve automatically managed watermark blocks. Include `LLM Usage` and any actually required ICLR checklist, not an invented checklist borrowed from another venue.\n\n### Forbidden Headings\n\nSearch Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Iteration History; Experiment A; Experiment B; Winner Selection; From Failure to Success; Latent-Count Bridge Results; Quantile-Stump Ablation.\n\n### Forbidden Phrases\n\n“we then tried”; “subsequent improvements”; “first valid result”; “next-best”; “later variants”; “search winner”; “correctness rate”; “correct and eligible”; “eligible”; “combined_score”; “rubric”; “worker_01”; “worker_02”; “state of the art”; “universally reliable”; “uniformly smaller regions”; “guaranteed coverage under covariate shift”; “distribution-free conditional validity”; “quantile stumps caused the gain”; “the latent bridge restored coverage”; “unscored attempts were measured losses”.\n\n@Article{Lei2016DistributionFreePI,\n author = {Jing Lei and Max G'Sell and A. Rinaldo and R. Tibshirani and L. Wasserman},\n booktitle = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n pages = {1094 - 1111},\n title = {Distribution-Free Predictive Inference for Regression},\n volume = {113},\n year = {2016}\n}\n\n@Article{Xu2022SequentialPC,\n author = {Chen Xu and Yao Xie},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Sequential Predictive Conformal Inference for Time Series},\n volume = {abs/2212.03463},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Harrison2024VariationalBL,\n author = {James Harrison and John Willes and Jasper Snoek},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Variational Bayesian Last Layers},\n volume = {abs/2404.11599},\n year = {2024}\n}\n\n@Article{Kivaranovic2019AdaptiveDP,\n author = {D. Kivaranovic and Kory D. Johnson and H. Leeb},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics},\n pages = {4346-4356},\n title = {Adaptive, Distribution-Free Prediction Intervals for Deep Networks},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Article{Bian2022TrainingconditionalCF,\n author = {Michael Y. Bian and R. F. Barber},\n booktitle = {Electronic Journal of Statistics},\n journal = {Electronic Journal of Statistics},\n title = {Training-conditional coverage for distribution-free predictive inference},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Solari2021MultiSC,\n author = {A. Solari and Vera Djordjilovi'c},\n booktitle = {Statistics and Probability Letters},\n journal = {Statistics & Probability Letters},\n title = {Multi split conformal prediction},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Gui2023ConformalizedMC,\n author = {Yu Gui and R. F. Barber and Cong Ma},\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n journal = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 36},\n title = {Conformalized matrix completion},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Gonçalves2017DynamicQL,\n author = {Kelly C. M. Gonçalves and H. Migon and L. Bastos},\n booktitle = {Bayesian Analysis},\n journal = {Bayesian Analysis},\n title = {Dynamic Quantile Linear Models: A Bayesian Approach},\n year = {2017}\n}\n\n@Article{Yu2020NonparametricEA,\n author = {Xiufan Yu and Jiawei Yao and Lingzhou Xue},\n booktitle = {Journal of business & economic statistics},\n journal = {Journal of Business & Economic Statistics},\n pages = {342 - 354},\n title = {Nonparametric Estimation and Conformal Inference of the Sufficient Forecasting With a Diverging Number of Factors},\n volume = {40},\n year = {2020}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Fong2024BayesianQE,\n author = {Edwin Fong and Andrew Yiu},\n title = {Bayesian Quantile Estimation and Regression with Martingale Posteriors},\n year = {2024}\n}\n\n@Article{Lee2021DistributionfreeIF,\n author = {Yonghoon Lee and Rina Foygel Barber},\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n pages = {7448-7459},\n title = {Distribution-free inference for regression: discrete, continuous, and in between},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Sit2019GaussianPR,\n author = {Hilarie Sit and C. Earls},\n booktitle = {Radio Science},\n journal = {Radio Science},\n title = {Gaussian Process Regression for Estimating EM Ducting Within the Marine Atmospheric Boundary Layer},\n volume = {55},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Gan2022LOCOFI,\n author = {Luqin Gan and Li-Li Zheng and G. Allen},\n title = {LOCO Feature Importance Inference without Data Splitting via Minipatch Ensembles},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Araz2025AnotherFB,\n author = {Jack Y. Araz and M. Spannowsky},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Another Fit Bites the Dust: Conformal Prediction as a Calibration Standard for Machine Learning in High-Energy Physics},\n volume = {abs/2512.17048},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Katsouris2023StructuralBD,\n author = {Christis Katsouris},\n title = {Structural Break Detection in Quantile Predictive Regression Models with Persistent Covariates},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Batra2023ConformalPF,\n author = {Devesh Batra and Salvatore Mercuri and Raad Khraishi},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Conformal Predictions for Longitudinal Data},\n volume = {abs/2310.02863},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Kepler2021WassersteinSplittingGP,\n author = {Michael E. Kepler and Alec Koppel and Amrit Singh Bedi and D. Stilwell},\n booktitle = {IEEE/RJS International Conference on Intelligent RObots and Systems},\n journal = {2021 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS)},\n pages = {9833-9840},\n title = {Wasserstein-Splitting Gaussian Process Regression for Heterogeneous Online Bayesian Inference},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Lin2022DistributionfreePI,\n author = {You-Wu Lin and Congcong Xu and Zhao-Jun Zhou and Liang Shen and Shuai Huang},\n booktitle = {Journal of Chemometrics},\n journal = {Journal of Chemometrics},\n title = {Distribution‐free predictive inference for partial least squares regression with applications to molecular descriptors datasets},\n volume = {36},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Manna2025DistributionfreeIF,\n author = {Alokesh Manna and A. Sett and D. Dey and Yuwen Gu and E. Schifano and Ji-Chao He},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Distribution-free inference for LightGBM and GLM with Tweedie loss},\n volume = {abs/2507.06921},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Kondratyev2025NeuralOT,\n author = {Vladimir Kondratyev and Alexander Fishkov and N. Kotelevskii and Mahmoud Hegazy and Rémi Flamary and Maxim Panov and Éric Moulines},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Neural Optimal Transport Meets Multivariate Conformal Prediction},\n volume = {abs/2509.25444},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Yi2025DistributionFreePS,\n author = {Menghan Yi and Yan-Lin Tang and H. J. Wang},\n title = {Distribution-Free Prediction Sets for Regression under Target Shift},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Manna2025ConformalPI,\n author = {Alokesh Manna and Aditya Vikram Sett and D. Dey and Yuwen Gu and Elizabeth D. Schifano and Ji-Chao He},\n booktitle = {Applied Stochastic Models in Business and Industry},\n journal = {Applied Stochastic Models in Business and Industry},\n title = {Conformal Prediction Inference in Regularized Insurance Models},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Kılıç2018RobustIF,\n author = {Rehim Kılıç},\n journal = {Econometric Reviews},\n pages = {1067 - 1094},\n title = {Robust inference for predictability in smooth transition predictive regressions},\n volume = {37},\n year = {2018}\n}\n\n@Article{Dabrowski2022BayesianNN,\n author = {J. Dabrowski and D. Pagendam},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Bayesian Neural Network Inference via Implicit Models and the Posterior Predictive Distribution},\n volume = {abs/2209.02188},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Yu2026FIREMR,\n author = {Rosen Yu and Nicholas Sung and Faez Ahmed},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {FIRE: Multi-fidelity Regression with Distribution-conditioned In-context Learning using Tabular Foundation Models},\n volume = {abs/2601.22371},\n year = {2026}\n}\n{\n  \"global_memory_dir\": \"work/results/r02/codex/20261112_215054/investigation\",\n  \"output_dir\": \"work/results/r02/codex/20261112_215054/paper\",\n  \"results_dir\": \"work/results/r02/codex/20261112_215054/investigation\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"task_dir\": \"work/results/r02/codex/20261112_215054/task\"\n}\n.codex/skills/memory-log/scripts:\nappend_memory.py\n\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts:\nopenalex_fetch.py\ns2_fetch.py\nscholarly_fetch.py\n\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts:\nscientific_evidence.py\nscientific_evidence.sh\n\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts:\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\n\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts:\nvalidate_claims.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
