{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_91","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: build_figures.py\n@@\n-                    font_sizes=dict(title=10, label=9, tick=8, legend=8))\n+                    font_sizes=dict(title=10, axis=9, ticks=8, legend=8))\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-Finally, the available code makes the scale-selection design inspectable, but documentary linkage to the frozen selection is not an independently recomputed source-tree identity proof. We preserve the stored provenance identifiers without treating them as substantive evidence for implementation details. Missing component diagnostics, failed extensions, and the lack of a matched final learned-ridge comparator limit mechanism-level attribution. These constraints narrow the contribution to an empirical finding about one fully specified evaluation, not a universally preferable compositional uncertainty estimator.\n+Readable source evidence supports the implementation description, but its documentary linkage is not an independently recomputed frozen source-tree identity proof. Missing component diagnostics and the matched final learned-ridge comparator limit mechanism-level attribution; supplementary material records source locations and incomplete extensions.\n@@\n-The broader methodological lesson is to distinguish three objects: the marginal guarantee supplied by calibration, the allocation of region size supplied by the fitted score, and the empirical diagnostics used to judge usefulness. A scalar utility can summarize a declared tradeoff but cannot replace that separation. Further work should test anisotropic or misspecified responses, real compositions, alternate reference measures, and successfully controlled component comparisons. Until such evidence exists, the supported claim remains condition-dependent usefulness of the complete frozen scale procedure, not quantile-stump superiority, conditional validity, or universal efficiency.\n+The methodological lesson is to distinguish the marginal guarantee supplied by calibration, the allocation of region size supplied by the score, and empirical usefulness. Utility cannot replace that separation. Anisotropic responses, real compositions, alternative reference measures, and controlled component comparisons remain open tests. The supported claim is condition-dependent usefulness of the complete scale procedure, not quantile-stump superiority or universal efficiency.\n*** Update File: references.bib\n@@\n @book{Vovk2005,\n+  author = {Vladimir Vovk and Alex Gammerman and Glenn Shafer},\n   title = {Algorithmic Learning in a Random World},\n*** Update File: prepare_references.py\n@@\n         authors = [' '.join(filter(None, [author.get('given'), author.get('family')]))\n                    for author in metadata.get('author', [])]\n+        if not authors:\n+            authors_url = 'https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/' + identifier\n+            authors_response = requests.get(authors_url, timeout=60)\n+            authors_response.raise_for_status()\n+            authors = [item['author']['display_name'] for item in authors_response.json()['authorships']]\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python build_figures.py\npython build_claims.py\nbibtex paper > /tmp/bibtex-paper.log 2>&1; pdflatex -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > /tmp/latex2.log 2>&1; pdflatex -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > /tmp/latex3.log 2>&1\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json,jsonschema,pymupdf\njsonschema.validate(json.load(open('figure_manifest.json')),json.load(open('.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json')));print('figure schema passed')\np=pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\nfor index,page in enumerate(p):\n text=page.get_text();print(index+1,len(text),text[-100:].replace('\\\\n',' '))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
